# The Borrow Contract Ledger of Nikaia's standard library.
#
# Part III 13.5 says a published package ships its ledger, so that a consumer
# builds against contracts rather than guesses. This is that file for `std`, and
# it is the one a program's compiler reads when the program calls `io::…` or
# `fs::…` - what a caller has to know about a function it cannot see the body
# of: whether it may pause (`sync`), whether it may fail and with what
# (`throws`), and what its result may point into (`returns`).
#
# `throws = ["?"]` on every entry here is not a placeholder but the honest
# answer: these functions fail with Rust's errors, which have no Nikaia type to
# name (ADR-023 D1, and ADR-024 D1's `?` - the absence of a claim). A caller
# reads it as "it fails", which is what the boolean before it said.
#
# **Two origins, and the difference is not hidden.** The entries for modules
# written in Nikaia are *inferred* by the Stage 0 compiler from their source,
# and `crates/nikaia/tests/contracts.rs` regenerates them and fails if this file
# has drifted. The entries for the modules that are still Rust cannot be
# inferred by a compiler that does not read Rust, so they are **written here and
# reviewed like code**. That is the honest shape of a library whose
# implementation is half in another language, and the share that is written by
# hand shrinks as more of `std` becomes Nikaia (ADR-014).
#
# An absent `sync` means *not* sync - which is the promise this file exists to
# make. An absent *entry* means nothing is known, and a caller may not assume.
#
# **Present, `sync` says which kind of promise it is** (ADR-027). `sync = true`
# is one the source made and the compiler checked; `sync = "inferred"` is one
# the body implies, derived from the calls it makes. A caller uses them
# identically - both mean "this cannot pause" - and a *diff* must not, because
# withdrawing a promise someone wrote is a decision and losing an inferred one
# is a consequence of an edit somewhere else. Every entry below that is written
# by hand is `true` by definition: a promise about a Rust body, made here.
#
# **A parameter whose Rust type is a bound rather than a type is recorded `?`.**
# `fs::write(path: impl AsRef<Path>, data: impl AsRef<[u8]>)` accepts a `String`
# and a `&str` and a buffer, so writing `&str` here would make the type checker
# reject `fs::write(path, text)` for a `String` - a correct program, refused.
# `?` is the absence of a claim (ADR-024 D1) and it is the true one: the arity
# is still checked, and the type is not claimed because there is no one type.
#
# `touches` says which resources a function reaches and whether it changes them
# (ADR-033, Part I 8.1.1). It is what lets two operations that meet on nothing
# run at the same time. **Absent means it touches everything**, which is the
# opposite of how an absent `throws` reads and is deliberate: an effect nobody
# described has to order against everything, or a missing contract would make a
# program wrong rather than merely slow. Only the entries below say it; every
# other function in this file keeps the order it was written in.
#
# The resources it may name are a **closed list** - `file(path)`, `stdin`,
# `stdout`, `stderr`, `args` - kept in `contracts::touch`. It is closed because
# two touches of different kinds never conflict, so a kind the compiler did not
# know would be disjoint from every kind there is: a typo here would buy an
# overlap rather than fail to buy one. A word from outside the list is answered
# the way ADR-033 D4 answers everything it cannot read - the function reaches
# everything and keeps its place - and the list grows when a function needs it
# (ADR-028 D5), never before.
#
# **`touches = []` is a claim about resources and not about memory.** A function
# that changes something it was handed - `Vec::push`, `String::push_str`,
# `HashMap::entry` - reaches no *resource*, and writing `touches = []` on it
# would still be true in ADR-033 D2's terms and wrong to write: the ordering
# analysis finds a data dependency by comparing what a statement binds against
# what the next one mentions, and `xs.push(a)` twice binds nothing and mentions
# `xs` in both. Memory is the borrow checker's question (ADR-033 D3) and this
# analysis does not have its answer, so the functions that change what they are
# given say nothing here and keep their order. The same goes for a function
# that runs a lambda - `Vec::sort_by_key`, `Entry::and_modify` - where a
# `touches = []` would be a claim about somebody else's code.
#
# **And `sync`'s answer to that does not transfer here.** `sync = "from(f)"`
# (ADR-029 D3) lets a higher-order function say "the lambda decides", sound
# because `sync` is a fact about a whole *function* and the lambda's body is
# walked as part of the function that writes it. `touches` is consumed per
# **statement**, and nothing walks a lambda for an effect - so "the lambda
# decides", read as "this call adds nothing", would buy an overlap on an
# incomplete touch set. There is no `touches = "from(f)"`, and the entry a
# higher-order function would need says *add* what the lambda reaches rather
# than assume it away (ADR-029 D3, and `contracts::order`'s `Expr::Closure`
# arm).
#
# `crosses` on a `[type."…"]` says a value of that type **may cross a thread**
# (ADR-005 §1 Group B, `NK25xx`). It is only ever written for a type whose parts
# are Rust: a Nikaia `struct` records its `fields` and the check walks those,
# structurally, which is what that record asks for. Its absence is "nobody said"
# and not "it may not" - and "nobody said" is not permission, so the compiler
# will not put such a value on a thread of its own choosing.
#
# `sharing` on a `[fn."…"]` says which of a function's `Shared` positions are one
# allocation and which reference count each of those classes gets (ADR-037 D7).
# No `std` entry has one, because no `std` signature mentions `Shared` - and
# `a_std_entry_that_takes_a_shared_needs_a_second_look` in
# `crates/nikaia/tests/sharing.rs` is what makes somebody look on the day one
# does: the analysis treats a described callee as accounted for, so an entry that
# could keep a handle and cross a thread with it is a decision and not a line.
#
# `provenance` marks a **source**: a function whose result is bytes that entered
# the program from outside, and who chose them (ADR-010 D2). Its absence is not
# "trusted" - it is "this is not a source", which is what almost every function
# is. Every source `std` has today is the operator's own: a file they named, a
# pipe they connected, the arguments they typed. The untrusted ones - `http`,
# `net`, `db` - arrive with the modules that have them.
version = 2
toolchain = "nikaia 0.1.0"
inference = "stage0-signatures+sync-bodies+throws-bodies+sharing-bodies"

# --- Written by hand: the modules that are still Rust ------------------------

# The arguments the program was started with. Read-only and read by anyone:
# two `cli::args()` calls hand back the same vector, so they meet on nothing
# and neither has to wait for the other. It is the most-refused callee in
# `examples/` - ten adjacent pairs named it - and it was refused for no better
# reason than that nobody had written this line.
[fn."cli::args"]
pub = true
touches = ["args read"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> Args"

[fn."eprint"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stderr write"]
signature = "(text: ?)"

[fn."eprintln"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stderr write"]
signature = "(text: ?)"

[fn."fs::map"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ?) -> Mapped"

[fn."fs::Mapped::deref"]
pub = true
sync = true
returns = "borrows(self)"
signature = "(&Mapped) -> &str"

[fn."fs::read"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ?) -> Vec[u8]"

[fn."fs::read_to_string"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) read"]
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "(path: ?) -> String"

[fn."fs::write"]
pub = true
touches = ["file(path) write"]
throws = ["?"]
signature = "(path: ?, data: ?; append: bool = false, create: bool = true)"

[fn."print"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stdout write"]
signature = "(text: ?)"

[fn."println"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = ["stdout write"]
signature = "(text: ?)"

[fn."html::escape"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
returns = "borrows(text)"
signature = "(text: &str) -> ?"

[fn."io::read"]
pub = true
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> Vec[u8]"

[fn."io::lines"]
pub = true
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> Lines"

[fn."io::read_to_string"]
pub = true
throws = ["?"]
provenance = "trusted"
signature = "() -> String"

[fn."list::ListExt::map"]
pub = true
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(?, f: fn(?)) -> ?"

# --- Written by hand: the collections and text the language below provides ---
#
# `Vec`, `HashMap`, `str` and `String` are Rust's, reached through Nikaia's
# spellings, and a compiler that does not read Rust cannot infer them. They are
# here because ADR-028 gave them a second job: the type checker resolves
# `xs.push(x)` only if it knows what `xs` is, and the `sync` inference (ADR-027)
# can only vouch for a method call the checker resolved. Every one of these was
# **observed being asked for** by a program in this repository; none is here
# speculatively, because a wrong entry does not merely fail to help - it makes
# the checker reject a correct program.
#
# Types are `?` wherever the honest answer is "no one type" (ADR-024 D1). What
# an entry does claim is its **arity**, which is checked - so the risk of being
# wrong here is real and the corpus is what holds it down.
#
# **A signature may name its receiver's type arguments** (ADR-031): `$V` in
# `HashMap::entry` is whatever the map holds, bound at the call site and
# substituted away. A variable that stays unbound becomes `?`, never a name.
# The rule for where one may appear is narrow and deliberate: **in what flows
# out** - a result, a lambda's parameter - and never in an argument. What flows
# out is a promise this file makes and is wrong on its own account; what flows
# in is a constraint on somebody's program, and Rust's collections take more
# than their type parameters suggest (`get` by anything the key borrows as), so
# a variable there would reject correct code.
#
# **A higher-order method says `sync = "from(f)"`.** Whether `and_modify` pauses
# depends on the lambda it is given, and the ledger can say "it depends" since
# ADR-029 D3: `from(f)` names the parameter that decides, and a caller reads it
# as "this call adds no pausing of its own". `sync = true` here would be a
# promise about somebody else's code, and before `from` existed the honest
# alternative was to say nothing and cost every caller its inferred `sync`.
# The bound is ADR-029 D4: `from` is for a lambda that runs *before the call
# returns*, because that is the only case whose calls the caller has already
# counted. `a_detached_lambda_may_not_use_from` holds every `from` entry in this
# file to it.
#
# It does not generalise to the other columns. `throws` needs nothing - a
# lambda's failures are already in the enclosing function's error set, by the
# same walk - and `touches` must not be read this way at all, for the reason the
# header's note on `touches = []` gives.

[fn."f64::abs"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(f64) -> f64"

[fn."f64::sqrt"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(f64) -> f64"

[fn."HashMap::entry"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[$K, $V], key: ?) -> Entry[$V]"

# `key: ?` and not `key: $K`, deliberately. Rust's `get` takes anything the key
# borrows as, so `m.get("x")` on a `HashMap[String, …]` is correct and `$K`
# would reject it. The rule is ADR-031's: a variable says what flows *out*, and
# `?` stays for what flows *in*.
[fn."HashMap::get"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[$K, $V], key: ?) -> $V"

[fn."HashMap::keys"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[?, ?]) -> ?"

[fn."HashMap::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[?, ?]) -> usize"

[fn."HashMap::new"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "() -> HashMap[?, ?]"

[fn."HashMap::values"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&HashMap[?, ?]) -> ?"

# Runs the lambda it is given, so what it does is what the lambda does - which
# is what `from(f)` says (ADR-029). Sound because the lambda runs *during* the
# call: its body belongs to the function that writes it, and its calls are
# counted there. A method that **stored** its lambda could not say this.
[fn."Entry::and_modify"]
pub = true
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(Entry[$V], f: fn(&$V)) -> Entry[$V]"

[fn."Entry::or_insert"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(Entry[$V], value: ?) -> &$V"

# Also runs its lambda, before it returns.
[fn."Entry::or_insert_with"]
pub = true
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(Entry[$V], f: fn()) -> &$V"

# `String::new()` reaches nothing and hands back an empty one. Four adjacent
# pairs in `examples/` named it as the callee nobody had described, which is
# what asked for the entry.
[fn."String::new"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "() -> String"

[fn."String::chars"]
pub = true
sync = true
returns = "borrows(self)"
signature = "(&String) -> ?"

[fn."String::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&String) -> usize"

[fn."String::push_str"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&String, text: ?)"

[fn."Vec::is_empty"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&Vec[?]) -> bool"

[fn."Vec::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&Vec[?]) -> usize"

[fn."Vec::new"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "() -> Vec[?]"

[fn."Vec::push"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&Vec[$T], value: ?)"

[fn."Vec::sort"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&Vec[?])"

[fn."Vec::sort_by_key"]
pub = true
sync = "from(f)"
signature = "(&Vec[$T], f: fn(&$T)) -> ?"

[fn."str::chars"]
pub = true
sync = true
returns = "borrows(self)"
signature = "(&str) -> ?"

[fn."str::len"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&str) -> usize"

[fn."str::repeat"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&str, n: ?) -> String"

[fn."str::starts_with"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(&str, prefix: ?) -> bool"

[fn."str::to_string"]
pub = true
sync = true
touches = []
signature = "(&str) -> String"

[fn."str::trim"]
pub = true
sync = true
returns = "borrows(self)"
signature = "(&str) -> &str"

# --- Inferred from `src/*.nika` ----------------------------------------------

[fn."text::digit_value"]
pub = true
sync = true
signature = "(c: char) -> i32"

# What the program was started with, and the one type here that says it **may
# cross a thread** (`crosses`, ADR-005 §1 Group B). Its parts are Rust, so
# `contracts::send` cannot walk them and would answer "nobody said" - which is
# not permission, and would have cost `cli::args` the overlapping that the
# `touches` line above bought it (ADR-033 §8.1). The claim is a promise about a
# Rust type, written here and reviewed like code, exactly as `sync = true` is.
[type."cli::Args"]
pub = true
crosses = true

[type."fs::Mapped"]
pub = true

# A step of this one can **fail**, and that is a fact a caller cannot get from
# anywhere else: a `for` over it is a place the enclosing function can fail
# from, and the compiler makes that function say `throws` (ADR-025 D1, `NK2701`).
[type."io::Lines"]
pub = true
iterates = "throws"
